          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly presents her love for the little prince as a current emotional state. The surrounding explanation that he had failed to know it makes this an explicit, corrective avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not undertake a future action, obligation, or relationship. Although the surrounding turn includes wishes and instructions concerning his departure, those are separate from the avowal and do not convert “je t'aime” into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression is represented as a calm, intelligible disclosure rather than a reflex-like discharge of affect. The scene contains strong emotion, including concealed crying, but that does not make the focal avowal itself an expressive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately captured as an avowal of a loving state. Its contextual role in correcting the little prince's misunderstanding does not constitute a separate core mode beyond T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At the little prince's departure, after apologizing for her previous conduct, the rose calmly tells him that she loves him and explains that her own fault prevented him from knowing it. The declaration functions as a belated disclosure and correction before their separation.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no undertaking. In the surrounding turn, the rose releases the little prince from tending her and urges him to be happy and leave, but these are separate directives or permissions, not promises embedded in “je t'aime.” The supplied scene shows his surprise and confusion but no explicit verbal acceptance or rejection of the avowal.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The literary narration strongly supports genuine affection through references to the rose's tenderness and concealed tears. Nevertheless, the classification concerns the represented force of the marked words, not a general judgment about the entire relationship, and no commitment should be imported from surrounding or later passages.\"}"
        }
